# Normalize every text file to LF, regardless of checkout platform —
# `* text=auto` keeps Git's own binary-vs-text autodetection (so real
# binaries aren't touched), `eol=lf` is what actually forces the LF
# normalization once a file's been classified as text.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true, the Git for
# Windows default) silently converts text files to CRLF — every line of
# every touched file then shows as "changed" in git diff/status even
# with zero real content changes, and `git archive` (what
# scripts/build-update-package.sh signs and ships) would include
# whatever line endings happen to be committed, not necessarily LF.
*            text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts specifically, called out beyond the general rule above
# for why it actually matters here, not just for clean diffs: they're
# executed directly — as a Docker ENTRYPOINT, and as systemd ExecStart
# on a provisioned appliance. A CRLF shebang line ("#!/bin/sh\r") makes
# the kernel look for an interpreter that doesn't exist, surfacing as
# "no such file or directory" even though the file is genuinely there.
*.sh         text eol=lf
